  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Pluteus aurantiorugosus, commonly known as the flame shield, is a small woodland mushroom characterized by its distinctive orange-red cap and wrinkled surface texture. This saprophytic fungus belongs to the Pluteaceae family and is found decomposing wood throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Texture",
      "description": "Bright orange-red to reddish-brown cap with distinctive radial wrinkles or ridges radiating from the center",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Free Gills",
      "description": "White to pale pink gills that are free from the stem and lack attachment to the cap's center",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pale Stem",
      "description": "Smooth, whitish to pale gray stem that is typically fragile and hollow",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species grows on decaying hardwood logs, stumps, and woody debris in forests and wooded areas throughout New England, typically appearing from spring through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The flame shield's wrinkled cap pattern resembles shield-shaped armor, giving it its common name and distinguishing it from the smoother caps of related Pluteus species.",
